      "math_academy_id": "q-98093",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)+⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-3,\\,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)+⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-3,\\,-4⟩+{⟨t,\\,t,\\,t⟩|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-3,\\,-4⟩+⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\text{ } \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-2,\\,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
